.TH HTTP_CANWRITE 3
.SH NAME
http_canwrite \- handle a writable socket of an HTTP client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <http.h>
.P
.BI "ssize_t http_canwrite(http* " h ", void (*" wantread ")(fd_type), void (*" wantwrite ")(fd_type));"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR http_canwrite ()
is called when the event loop reports that the socket of
.I h
can be written. The first call completes the connection (the TLS handshake for https) and sends the request with
.BR http_sendreq (3).
Later calls write body data that was queued by
.BR http_chunk (3)
or did not fit before.
.P
.I wantread
and
.I wantwrite
are functions of the event loop, normally io_onlywantread() and io_onlywantwrite(); a TLS
handshake or write that has to wait for the other direction uses them to change what the
socket is watched for.
.SH RETURN VALUE
0 on success, -1 on an error. On -1 look at h->err: EAGAIN or EWOULDBLOCK means to wait for the next event, anything else is a failure of the connection.
.SH SEE ALSO
http(3), http_canread(3), http_chunk(3), http_sendreq(3)
